## Job System

As of March 9, 2026, there is currently one available job in the game. Additional jobs will be added as development continues.

The job system allows the player to earn money while balancing time, stress, and daily responsibilities. As the game world expands, new jobs will become 
available across multiple districts. This will give the player more opportunities to combine part-time jobs and maximize earnings.Different jobs will have 
different working hours and locations, encouraging the player to travel across the city. This also increases the chances of encountering events or obstacles 
that may impact the player's ability to reach work.

Current Job
Clothing Store (Implemented)

The player can interact with the cash register inside the clothing store to open the Work Menu, which allows them to select a shift.
Each shift advances the in-game clock, rewards the player with money, and increases the player's stress.

| Work Type | Time Worked | Money Earned | Stress Gained |
| --------- | ----------- | ------------ | ------------- |
| Part-Time | 4 hours     | $40          | +5            |
| Full Time | 8 hours     | $80          | +10           |
| Overtime  | 12 hours    | $120         | +20           |

Planned Jobs

Additional jobs will be added as development progresses. These jobs will provide new work schedules and income opportunities.
Planned roles include:
  - Personal Trainer (Gym)
  - Cashier (Corner Store)

Future jobs will be located in the same and different districts throughout the city to encourage exploration and create more gameplay variety.

Design Purpose

The job system supports the core gameplay loop by forcing the player to make decisions about how they spend their time each day.
Players must balance:
  - Working to earn money
  - Managing stress and morale
  - Taking care of personal responsibilities
  - Navigating a city that is gradually falling into dissent

As the game world evolves, external events may begin to affect jobs, such as protests, blocked roads, or business closures.
